  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Notably warm low-level thicknesses beneath \nanomalous ridging aloft will support above to well above normal \ntemperatures for much of ENC today. The one exception is the \nnorthern OBX where a slowing front may briefly dip into the area\nleading to a \"cooler\" northeasterly flow. \n\nIncreasing low-level moisture will support increasing humidity \ntoday which should translate to heat indices topping out at \n100- 105 degrees. Risk of heat indices exceeding 105F still\nappears too low to issue any heat advisory for today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Today, a slowing cold front will begin \nencroaching on ENC from Virginia. Guidance is honing in on a\nsolution where the front lingers somewhere between the NC/VA\nborder and the Pamlico river. Given the strength of the \nSoutheast U.S. ridge, it would make sense that the front won't \nmake much headway through ENC. Meanwhile, moisture and \ninstability will continue to increase south of the front, and \nthis should support at least a slight step up in the risk of \nafternoon convection. This may especially be the case as a \nshortwave moves through the area and as we get a glancing \ninfluence from the entrance region of an upper jet that will be \nsliding by to the north. While the focus of convection should be\nwhere the front stalls, it stands to reason that isolated to \nscattered convection will be able to develop, likely focused \nalong the seabreeze. Instability looks to be modest (around \n1000j/kg MLCAPE), but with some residually dry air aloft and \nhigher DCAPE, the strongest storms may produce gusty winds of \n40-50 mph. \n\nMoisture continues to build on Saturday, with PWATs forecast to\nrise above 2\". This combined with the seabreeze, a dampening \nshortwave, and inland troughing should support an increased \ncoverage of convection compared to Friday. There's some \nuncertainty regarding the strength of convergence along the \ninland trough. If the inland trough ends up weaker, the coverage\nof convection may be lower across the coastal plain. \nRegardless, any thunderstorms that form on Saturday will be \ncapable of very heavy rainfall rates and minor, nuisance-stlye \nflooding. Instability continues to look modest, and this should \nkeep the risk of strong to severe thunderstorms LOW. \n\nOn Sunday, the stalled front should finally get shoved south \nthrough all of ENC as a stronger shortwave passes by to the \nnorth. A continued moist and modestly unstable environment \nshould support another day of scattered to perhaps numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms along the advancing front. Heavy rain \nand minor flooding continues to look like the primary risk.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Anomalous upper level ridging and a notably \nstrong surface high builds into the Mid-Atlantic and Carolinas \nnext week favoring warm and dry conditions.",
